      "text_raw": "For a specific V −V , the\nGS T\ntotal transconductance is\neasily plotted versus channel\nlength L, as shown next.\nActually, both transcon-\nductances are plotted\nseparately.\nThe transconductance in\nsaturation is in blue; the one\nin strong inversion for\nV =0.2 V in black and\nGST\nfor V =0.5 V in red. For\nGST\nV =0.2 V, the crossover\nGST\nis around 65 nm channel\nlength. This means that\nnowadays for analog\ndesigns in 0.13 mm and 0.18 mm, we can still use the model in strong inversion and that a small\nfactor of h is sufficient to provide accurate values of transconductance.\nFor high-frequency applications however, in which V =0.5 V or higher, the crossover value\nGST\nof channel length is about 0.15 mm. Transistors in a 90 nm technology operate mainly in velocity\nsaturation. Parameter h is then much larger, and certainly cannot be neglected.",
      "text": "For a specific V −V , the GS T total transconductance is easily plotted versus channel length L, as shown next. Actually, both transconductances are plotted separately. The transconductance in saturation is in blue; the one in strong inversion for V =0.2 V in black and GST for V =0.5 V in red. For GST V =0.2 V, the crossover GST is around 65 nm channel length. This means that nowadays for analog designs in 0.13 mm and 0.18 mm, we can still use the model in strong inversion and that a small factor of h is sufficient to provide accurate values of transconductance. For high-frequency applications however, in which V =0.5 V or higher, the crossover value GST of channel length is about 0.15 mm. Transistors in a 90 nm technology operate mainly in velocity saturation. Parameter h is then much larger, and certainly cannot be neglected."
    }
  ],
